 $C.$" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558813 1072 #uuid "0938f21c-1f96-532e-9f2d-e53ff0ce4b9c" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558813 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(-1)+2=1, \\\\ y & =1(-1)^2-4=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558814 1080 "y=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{5}{2}" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558814 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558814 1083 #uuid "0a8c6fb1-ed90-53ee-b5fd-e3890426b5cc" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558815 1080 "\\frac{15}{2}" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558815 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558815 1083 #uuid "0c2c0c5f-3772-535a-b35c-39dcc0eb618a" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558816 1080 "y=11 - 2 x" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558816 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558816 1083 #uuid "1e23f241-f452-5243-a0b7-f77e541a0989" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558817 1080 "y=21 - 2 x" 1319413953602