infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558835 1072 #uuid "bc10b2ad-7055-5588-94f5-628af39c3546" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558835 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(1)+3=4, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2+2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(4,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{1}=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(8)}=- \\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(6) & =- \\frac{1}{8}(x-(4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558825 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558836 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t - 2,\\,y=4 t^{2} - 3,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558836 1072 #uuid "bf1b4822-c23a-58d8-a2b6-77ecfb6d4a0e" 13194139536027 17592186045443 true] [17592186558836 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)-2=2, \\